# Four files that every branch appends to, and nothing else.
#
# Adding one b7e tool means a new bin/b7e-X, a new lib/X.js and a new test/b7eX.mjs —
# none of which can conflict, because nothing else has ever seen them — plus one line
# in each of the four registries below. Those four lines are where the whole conflict
# load of this repo comes from.
#
# Measured 2026-08-26 (bc-8479e) by merging all 34 open pull request branches onto
# origin/main in a scratch clone, in the order they would reach the head of the queue:
# 13 of the 34 conflicted. lib/grants.js in 10 of them, lib/toolbelt.js in 5,
# package.json and package-lock.json in 2, README.md in 3 — and lib/server.js in
# exactly one, which is the only conflict in the whole set where the two sides had
# anything to say to each other. Everything else was both sides adding a line.
#
# Ten of the fourteen open branches touching lib/grants.js insert at the same line, 333,
# after the last b7e grant. So the moment any one of them lands the other nine conflict —
# not by bad luck, but because there is only one place to put the line.
#
# `merge=union` is git's built-in driver for exactly this: where both sides added lines
# in the same place, keep both, in that order, instead of stopping. Re-running the same
# 34-branch simulation with these four lines took the conflicts from 13 to 4, and the
# four survivors are all README.md.
#
# The tree it produced was right rather than merely parseable: 112 bin entries with no
# duplicate key anywhere, `node --check` clean on both registries, and test/grants.mjs,
# test/loadorder.mjs, test/lockfile.mjs and test/anchors.mjs all green over it.
#
# What union costs, and where the cost is paid: it keeps both sides even when the two
# sides *edited* one line rather than appending to it. Two branches changing a grant from
# read to write would leave the key twice and the last one winning, in silence. That is
# what test/unionmerge.mjs is for — it fails the repo on a duplicate entry in any of the
# four, so the one case union gets wrong is the one case that is now loud. Do not add a
# file here without adding it there.
#
# See "Landing work — a branch, a pull request, and a merge queue" in README.md.

lib/grants.js      merge=union
lib/toolbelt.js    merge=union
package.json       merge=union
package-lock.json  merge=union

# README.md is deliberately NOT here, and it is the file that conflicts most often.
#
# It is 2.6MB and 36,601 lines, and all of it is prose. Union's whole trade is that it
# resolves an append silently — which is right for a list, where a duplicated entry is
# visible to a scan and now fails a suite, and wrong for a paragraph, where a section
# quietly appearing twice reads as writing and nothing anywhere would catch it. A
# conflict in a document is a question worth stopping on.
#
# It also does not need it much. The b7e sections append at scattered anchors between
# roughly lines 18,800 and 21,600 rather than at one shared line, so most of them merge
# clean on their own — 3 of 34, against lib/grants.js's 10.
